<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gang of Four</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gang of Four was the radical faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> that rose to power during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a>, Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen. They were arrested on 6 October 1976, a month after Mao's death, blamed for the excesses of the Cultural Revolution and sentenced after a public trial in 1980–1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gang-of-four.jpg" alt="Gang of Four" width="320" height="80" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: China Publications Centre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Gang_of_Four%22_on_trial_in_1980.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Gang of Four was a Maoist faction of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> officials who came to prominence during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> of 1966-1976. Its leading figure was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a>, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, a former film actress who had taken no public political role before 1966 and whom Mao placed in charge of the country's cultural apparatus. Zhang Chunqiao, Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen were party leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> who had played leading roles in securing that city for Mao. Yao Wenyuan's article of 10 November 1965 attacking the play Hai Rui Dismissed from Office is cited as the spark of the Cultural Revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> began to lose momentum around the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> in 1971. Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> regained his authority and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> back into the leadership at the 10th Party Congress in 1973. Near the end of Mao's life a power struggle developed between the Gang and an alliance of Deng, Zhou and Ye Jianying. After Zhou died in January 1976, the Gang used their newspapers to attack Deng and mobilised their urban militia units. It remains unclear which decisions were Mao's and which the Gang's own.</p><p>Mao died on 9 September 1976. Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a> attacked the radicals' media line at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> meeting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a> insisted that she be named party chairman. Hua won the army over to his side, and on 6 October 1976 he had the four leading radicals arrested in a bloodless coup. Nationwide denunciations began on 21 October. Government media blamed the Gang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a> for the excesses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, and many Chinese saw the arrests as the sign of a new era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The four were tried in late 1980 by the Supreme People's Court, with Jiang Hua presiding, and convicted in January 1981 of anti-party activities. The trial was broadcast daily on television. The prosecution separated political errors from crimes, among them the usurpation of state power and the persecution of some 750,000 people, 34,375 of whom died in 1966-1976. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a> alone argued her own case, saying she had obeyed Mao at all times; Zhang Chunqiao admitted nothing, while Yao Wenyuan and Wang Hongwen expressed repentance.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a> and Zhang Chunqiao received death sentences later commuted to life imprisonment, Wang Hongwen life, and Yao Wenyuan twenty years. Jiang Qing died by suicide in 1991 and Wang Hongwen died in 1992; Yao Wenyuan and Zhang Chunqiao died in 2005. The term was later used for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hua-guofeng">Hua Guofeng</a>'s four closest collaborators, who were demoted in 1980 for grave errors in the struggle against the Gang.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gang of Four” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Mao’s death in September 1976 the <b>Gang of Four</b>, led by his widow Jiang Qing, was arrested, and from the Third Plenum of December 1978 Deng Xiaoping was the paramount leader.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Gang of Four</b> fell in 1976 he was detained for investigation, stripped of all his posts and expelled from the party, and questioned about his ties to Lin Biao and Jiang Qing; his membership was restored in 1987.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
